But as I mentioned to you,
and as my listeners know,

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:21.420
we always start out just
doing a general check-in.

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:23.460
So obviously there's a lot that continues

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:25.860
to happen in our country
and just wanted to start

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:27.450
and have you share with our listeners

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:29.700
how you're doing at this point in time,

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:32.310
especially with all the
roles that you have.

00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:34.503
- Well, thank you so
much for the question.

00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:40.730
Yeah, it's a heavy time in our country,

00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:43.200
in various parts of the country,

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:45.030
really over all over the world.

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:50.030
There are so many challenging,
tragic, horrific incidences

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:56.840
of mass violence and it's
just a really heavy time.

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:00.870
And now that I'm based
in Rochester, New York,

00:04:00.870 --> 00:04:04.980
it's upstate just 60
minutes away from Buffalo.

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:09.720
So those things are still
present with me as it is

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:12.210
with so many of us and
trying to figure out

00:04:12.210 --> 00:04:17.210
how to not just be overwhelmed
by the grief and the trauma,

00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:23.070
but to be engaged in some form of action

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:27.150
to try and shift that so that
it doesn't feel as hopeless,

00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:29.343
and so that we don't feel as powerless.

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:33.150
So that's where I'm at.

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:35.490
- Well, I definitely appreciate
that level of honesty.

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:36.690
And, you know, as we've been talking

00:04:36.690 --> 00:04:41.190
to just that tension of trying
to navigate as a society too,

00:04:41.190 --> 00:04:43.786
so that we're not just
overwhelmed all the time.

00:04:43.786 --> 00:04:45.300
But again, you know,
of course we don't want

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:46.170
to ignore what's happening,

00:04:46.170 --> 00:04:47.970
but we don't want to
be stuck in it as well.

00:04:47.970 --> 00:04:51.810
So appreciate you just highlighting
that and letting us know

00:04:51.810 --> 00:04:53.670
how you're navigating that as well.

00:04:53.670 --> 00:04:56.790
As a follow up and I
guess somewhat related to,

00:04:56.790 --> 00:04:59.730
what does your day to day cadence
look like in your position

00:04:59.730 --> 00:05:01.170
for those who might not be as familiar

00:05:01.170 --> 00:05:04.530
with what an addiction
psychiatrist does, for instance,

00:05:04.530 --> 00:05:08.310
especially with everything else
that's going on already too?

00:05:08.310 --> 00:05:10.440
- So it really varies from day to day,

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:13.800
but I'll give sort of
the mix of activities.

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:17.580
So I'm seeing patients
and I can see patients

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:20.820
who have a primary substance use disorder.

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:25.650
So some days I'm getting
folks inducted on medications

00:05:25.650 --> 00:05:27.720
to treat their opioid use disorder

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:31.950
or to help with cravings for
cocaine use or nicotine use,

00:05:31.950 --> 00:05:33.033
things like that.

00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:37.020
And I may go from that
appointment to seeing someone

00:05:37.020 --> 00:05:40.110
who has both a combination
of substance use

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:41.400
and mental health concerns

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:44.430
and talking to them about
medications to address one

00:05:44.430 --> 00:05:45.993
or both of those things.

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:49.260
I'm also working with residents

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:51.270
who may be in the clinic rotating,

00:05:51.270 --> 00:05:52.980
and they may see a patient.

00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:54.480
And then we discuss the case,

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:57.090
and I give them some teaching points.

00:05:57.090 --> 00:05:59.073
Some days I'm doing a lecture.

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:04.440
Some days I am in administrative meetings,

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:06.140
actually that's probably every day

00:06:07.338 --> 00:06:09.120
that I'm in some sort of
administrative meeting,

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:10.980
both related to clinical work,

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:14.520
but also related to some
of the health equity work.

00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:16.920
That's really a passion for me,

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:18.570
and at the core of a lot of what I do.

00:06:18.570 --> 00:06:23.570
So the day varies, but
thinking about the patient

00:06:26.130 --> 00:06:28.890
and the systems that are
treating our patients,

00:06:28.890 --> 00:06:32.520
that's sort of at the core what I do.

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:33.657
- Well, I appreciate you
sharing that as well.

00:06:33.657 --> 00:06:35.850
And I think that gives
people, you know, a snapshot

00:06:35.850 --> 00:06:37.530
in a sense of some of those things.

00:06:37.530 --> 00:06:38.700
I think there's a lot of things there

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:41.550
that we can unpack as well in terms of

00:06:41.550 --> 00:06:43.800
how people enter into
the spaces where you are

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:46.106
and how you're also entering
in and trying to meet them

00:06:46.106 --> 00:06:47.550
where they are.

00:06:47.550 --> 00:06:48.383
But before we go there,

00:06:48.383 --> 00:06:49.890
I was wondering if you'd
actually just take people

00:06:49.890 --> 00:06:52.290
through your path as well.

00:06:52.290 --> 00:06:53.580
How did you get to this place

00:06:53.580 --> 00:06:55.620
of going into addiction psychiatry?

00:06:55.620 --> 00:06:57.810
Is it something that you
had an interest in early on?

00:06:57.810 --> 00:06:58.962
Is it something

00:06:58.962 --> 00:07:00.510
that came up a little
bit later down the road?

00:07:00.510 --> 00:07:02.730
Like what did that path look like for you?

00:07:02.730 --> 00:07:04.500
- Yeah, so it's really interesting.

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:07.380
Actually, it wasn't something

00:07:07.380 --> 00:07:09.390
that I thought I would
do from the beginning.

00:07:09.390 --> 00:07:12.720
And even my journey to psychiatry
was somewhat circuitous.

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:13.830
When I went to medical school,

00:07:13.830 --> 00:07:17.130
I didn't think that I'd
become a psychiatrist,

00:07:17.130 --> 00:07:19.920
but my rotation in third year

00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:23.010
on the psychiatry clerkship
really opened my eyes

00:07:23.010 --> 00:07:27.300
to the difference that could be made,

00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:32.300
and also just the importance
from a faith perspective,

00:07:32.700 --> 00:07:35.130
what's considered the
ministry of presence,

00:07:35.130 --> 00:07:37.590
what it means to actually
just sit with people

00:07:37.590 --> 00:07:41.460
and be present with them when
they're facing challenges.

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:46.460
And psychiatry creates an
opportunity to sit with folks

00:07:46.770 --> 00:07:50.400
when they're facing various
kinds of difficulties

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:52.950
and to work collaboratively with them on

00:07:52.950 --> 00:07:56.040
how to move forward
and take the next step.

00:07:56.040 --> 00:07:59.580
So that's how I got hooked in psychiatry.

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:02.040
And then a similar thing
happened to me in residency

00:08:02.040 --> 00:08:04.110
while I was going
through various rotations

00:08:04.110 --> 00:08:06.840
and I did my addiction
psychiatry rotation,

00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:11.840
and I realized that we can actually help,

00:08:11.880 --> 00:08:15.420
that treatments work for addiction

00:08:15.420 --> 00:08:17.610
and that there is
something that can be done.

00:08:17.610 --> 00:08:20.070
And when I'm meeting with someone again,

00:08:20.070 --> 00:08:22.950
feeling overwhelmed by the symptoms

00:08:22.950 --> 00:08:24.420
of their substance use disorder,

00:08:24.420 --> 00:08:28.563
overwhelmed by the consequences
of their substance use,

00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:33.330
that we can work
collaboratively to figure out

00:08:33.330 --> 00:08:35.250
what that next step will be for them.

00:08:35.250 --> 00:08:39.300
And seeing that there was
something that could be done

00:08:39.300 --> 00:08:42.450
and that there was hope
within the field of addiction,

00:08:42.450 --> 00:08:43.950
that really hooked me.

00:08:43.950 --> 00:08:47.970
And that's how I
continued on this journey.

00:08:47.970 --> 00:08:49.320
- Wow, that's really powerful

00:08:49.320 --> 00:08:52.380
and inspiring just to hear
about what pulled you in.

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:55.650
And I sense that that
continues to motivate you

00:08:55.650 --> 00:08:56.760
as you go forward too.

00:08:56.760 --> 00:08:58.560
But then even as you just
talked about, you know,

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:00.863
the ministry of presence and all of that,

00:09:00.863 --> 00:09:02.370
I mean, it's really powerful.

00:09:02.370 --> 00:09:05.280
And I can imagine that people are grateful

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:07.170
for that approach as well.

00:09:07.170 --> 00:09:08.850
You know, as you were talking
about just the treatments

00:09:08.850 --> 00:09:10.350
that are there and that
these things actually work,

00:09:10.350 --> 00:09:13.350
I'm also curious if people
come into your office

00:09:13.350 --> 00:09:15.540
or into treatment with that mindset,

00:09:15.540 --> 00:09:17.760
or if that's something
that you feel like you have

00:09:17.760 --> 00:09:20.070
to instill, or if it's a mixture.

00:09:20.070 --> 00:09:22.230
And we'll get into, you
know, some of the stigmas

00:09:22.230 --> 00:09:23.220
and things like that as well.

00:09:23.220 --> 00:09:24.840
But as you were talking,
that was just one question

00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:26.250
that came to mind.

00:09:26.250 --> 00:09:28.710
- Yeah, it is a mixture
and it all depends on

00:09:28.710 --> 00:09:31.533
where someone is in
their recovery journey.

00:09:32.520 --> 00:09:36.030
So I have patients who are
coming in for the first time

00:09:36.030 --> 00:09:39.000
and don't really know what to expect,

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:42.900
and, you know, we let them
know what the options are

00:09:42.900 --> 00:09:46.500
and let them know what the
evidence shows in terms

00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:49.080
of some of the treatments
that we have to offer

00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:52.410
and how effective they are and you know,

00:09:52.410 --> 00:09:55.350
what they can expect out of the process.

00:09:55.350 --> 00:09:57.060
And then I have other patients

00:09:57.060 --> 00:10:00.000
who have been in various
treatment programs,

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:02.400
whether it's inpatient or outpatient,

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:05.460
and they've done it so many times

00:10:05.460 --> 00:10:09.450
and they just feel like
I've tried everything

00:10:09.450 --> 00:10:11.763
and I'm not sure where to go from here.

00:10:12.810 --> 00:10:15.450
And that's a tough spot to be in.

00:10:15.450 --> 00:10:19.530
But I think it's also
important as a clinician

00:10:19.530 --> 00:10:22.170
to hold that space of hope even

00:10:22.170 --> 00:10:24.570
when the patient is feeling
like they're in a place

00:10:24.570 --> 00:10:25.800
that's hopeless.

00:10:25.800 --> 00:10:28.890
And so I'll sit with them
and in a similar way,

00:10:28.890 --> 00:10:31.050
I'll say, well, let's
go through the options

00:10:31.050 --> 00:10:33.240
because there may be a
combination of things

00:10:33.240 --> 00:10:35.160
that you haven't quite tried yet,

00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:37.350
or you're in a different
place in your life

00:10:37.350 --> 00:10:39.600
than you were in, in the past.

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:43.530
And so something might work
now that didn't work before.

00:10:43.530 --> 00:10:45.840
So it really exists along the spectrum.

00:10:45.840 --> 00:10:48.720
Some folks are well aware of the options,

00:10:48.720 --> 00:10:51.570
and feel like, you know, I'm not sure

00:10:51.570 --> 00:10:53.100
what can really happen here.

00:10:53.100 --> 00:10:55.440
And other folks are coming
in sort of brand new,

00:10:55.440 --> 00:10:58.050
trying to figure out how this
journey will look for them.

00:10:58.050 --> 00:11:01.950
So just meeting people where
they are and trying to maintain

00:11:01.950 --> 00:11:05.160
that space of hope while they have options

00:11:05.160 --> 00:11:07.500
to pursue their next steps.

00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:08.333
- That's great.

00:11:08.333 --> 00:11:09.840
I mean, I appreciate hearing
that you're meeting people

00:11:09.840 --> 00:11:10.989
where they are,

00:11:10.989 --> 00:11:12.420
and it sounds like you're
also having a dialogue.

00:11:12.420 --> 00:11:15.300
So it's not you just
disseminating information saying,

00:11:15.300 --> 00:11:17.880
this is what has to
happen, but really hearing,

00:11:17.880 --> 00:11:18.900
as you said, meeting them where they are

00:11:18.900 --> 00:11:19.830
and hearing what they're bringing

00:11:19.830 --> 00:11:22.074
into the conversation as well.

00:11:22.074 --> 00:11:23.988
I think that's helpful on lot of fronts.

00:11:23.988 --> 00:11:26.820
- Absolutely, there's
no way that it can only

00:11:26.820 --> 00:11:28.500
be a one-sided conversation.

00:11:28.500 --> 00:11:31.230
I mean, if you just think
about any doctor's appointment

00:11:31.230 --> 00:11:32.550
that you attend.

00:11:32.550 --> 00:11:35.622
If the doctor is the only
person doing the talking,

00:11:35.622 --> 00:11:38.130
they may suggest things
that actually just don't fit

00:11:38.130 --> 00:11:39.840
and work for your lifestyle.

00:11:39.840 --> 00:11:42.780
The best medical advice
that I've gotten myself is

00:11:42.780 --> 00:11:45.450
when my doctor is actually listening to me

00:11:45.450 --> 00:11:47.580
and hearing what my life is like,

00:11:47.580 --> 00:11:50.820
and then adjusting the
treatment recommendations based

00:11:50.820 --> 00:11:53.170
on things that might actually work for me.

00:11:53.170 --> 00:11:54.840
And I feel like that's the only way

00:11:54.840 --> 00:11:58.500
that we can really be successful
in any medical treatment,

00:11:58.500 --> 00:12:01.380
but especially in the
space of mental health

00:12:01.380 --> 00:12:02.987
and substance use.

00:12:02.987 --> 00:12:04.200
- Yeah, that's so important.

00:12:04.200 --> 00:12:06.390
It actually reminds me of,
you know, some of the comments

00:12:06.390 --> 00:12:08.820
that came up in the town hall
that we had in New York City.

00:12:08.820 --> 00:12:10.170
I remember Vicky Sigworth

00:12:10.170 --> 00:12:11.640
from the National
Alliance on Mental Illness

00:12:11.640 --> 00:12:14.010
in New Haven, just
talking about, you know,

00:12:14.010 --> 00:12:16.410
and this was much broader
than addiction psychiatry,

00:12:16.410 --> 00:12:18.000
but then when people are going in

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:20.700
to see anyone around mental health,

00:12:20.700 --> 00:12:24.180
whether the practitioners are
just telling them information

00:12:24.180 --> 00:12:25.950
or saying, tell me your story.

00:12:25.950 --> 00:12:27.660
It sounds like you're in that same mindset

00:12:27.660 --> 00:12:31.020
of what is your story,
what have you been through,

00:12:31.020 --> 00:12:32.010
what have you walked through?

00:12:32.010 --> 00:12:33.210
Where are you right now?

00:12:33.210 --> 00:12:34.043
What have you tried?

00:12:34.043 --> 00:12:34.876
What have you not tried?

00:12:34.876 --> 00:12:35.709
And trying to find that balance

00:12:35.709 --> 00:12:36.870
to have that two-way conversation,

00:12:36.870 --> 00:12:40.680
which I think is so
important on so many fronts.

00:12:40.680 --> 00:12:41.820
And then even as we think, you know,

00:12:41.820 --> 00:12:43.530
even outside or not outside,

00:12:43.530 --> 00:12:45.150
but in addition to the
psychiatry, you know,

00:12:45.150 --> 00:12:46.980
as I think about as a neuroscientist,

00:12:46.980 --> 00:12:49.083
I've had some of those
same experiences as well,

00:12:49.083 --> 00:12:50.580
even if I'm talking about, for instance,

00:12:50.580 --> 00:12:52.140
in Neuroscience of Addiction,

00:12:52.140 --> 00:12:53.850
an event that I did at public library.

00:12:53.850 --> 00:12:55.560
That was really a two-way conversation.

00:12:55.560 --> 00:12:58.860
It wasn't just me there
disseminating all the information,

00:12:58.860 --> 00:13:00.360
but it was helpful to hear from people

00:13:00.360 --> 00:13:03.600
what their experiences were
to see how that impacted

00:13:03.600 --> 00:13:05.580
how they interpreted what I was saying.

00:13:05.580 --> 00:13:08.310
And to also have them share,
well, I was on this medication,

00:13:08.310 --> 00:13:10.890
it made me feel this way,
what you're saying about

00:13:10.890 --> 00:13:12.450
what happens in the brain is also helpful.

00:13:12.450 --> 00:13:14.278
So just kind of a back and forth dialogue,

00:13:14.278 --> 00:13:17.160
where we're both able to
learn from each other.

00:13:17.160 --> 00:13:18.930
And I think everyone came away from that

00:13:18.930 --> 00:13:21.000
with a much better understanding,

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:23.340
even though it wasn't a
clinical setting per se,

00:13:23.340 --> 00:13:24.840
but I feel like that's just important

00:13:24.840 --> 00:13:26.010
for us to do as a society,

00:13:26.010 --> 00:13:29.190
so that we can have better
understanding from both sides,

00:13:29.190 --> 00:13:31.230
all sides and really have
a way to move forward.

00:13:31.230 --> 00:13:33.595
So it's really encouraging to hear.

00:13:33.595 --> 00:13:35.850
- I love that you did that
when it came to neuroscience,

00:13:35.850 --> 00:13:38.940
like that's really exciting

00:13:38.940 --> 00:13:41.970
because so often conversations related

00:13:41.970 --> 00:13:44.320
to whether it's mental
health or substance use,

00:13:46.860 --> 00:13:49.803
even the neuroscience of it
stays in a clinical space.

00:13:50.760 --> 00:13:53.820
There isn't a lot of
engagement in public spaces

00:13:53.820 --> 00:13:56.100
with the science of addiction

00:13:56.100 --> 00:13:58.800
or the science of mental health concerns.

00:13:58.800 --> 00:14:02.040
And so that's really impactful.

00:14:02.040 --> 00:14:05.730
And it sounds like it was also
empowering for individuals

00:14:05.730 --> 00:14:09.480
who were there to have the
science sort of presented

00:14:09.480 --> 00:14:11.640
to them in a way that's like digestible

00:14:11.640 --> 00:14:14.520
and that they can really
appreciate and understand.

00:14:14.520 --> 00:14:15.480
- Yeah, definitely.

00:14:15.480 --> 00:14:18.330
And it was a really important
reminder for me as well.

00:14:18.330 --> 00:14:19.950
I mean, I think there were perspectives

00:14:19.950 --> 00:14:21.780
that were changed from people who came in

00:14:21.780 --> 00:14:22.950
and also for me as well.

00:14:22.950 --> 00:14:26.040
Some people came in saying,
I don't see the addiction

00:14:26.040 --> 00:14:29.160
as an illness because
I did this to myself.

00:14:29.160 --> 00:14:29.993
- Yeah.

00:14:29.993 --> 00:14:31.740
- Which I always say, of course,
that's true at some level

00:14:31.740 --> 00:14:35.340
because you have to use
beforehand initially,

00:14:35.340 --> 00:14:37.320
if something is going to
transition to that point.

00:14:37.320 --> 00:14:39.420
But I think at the same time,
they also came away saying,

00:14:39.420 --> 00:14:42.390
okay, now I have a better understanding

00:14:42.390 --> 00:14:44.730
of what my use has done or is doing

00:14:44.730 --> 00:14:46.980
to my brain in terms of long term.

00:14:46.980 --> 00:14:48.360
And I also have a better understanding

00:14:48.360 --> 00:14:50.160
that that's not the end of the story,

00:14:50.160 --> 00:14:53.400
that there different ways that
yes, the brain has changed

00:14:53.400 --> 00:14:55.260
because of the exposure and use,

00:14:55.260 --> 00:14:56.550
but it can still continue to change.

00:14:56.550 --> 00:14:58.140
Our brain changes for all sorts of reasons

00:14:58.140 --> 00:14:59.640
and experiences that we go through.

00:14:59.640 --> 00:15:01.290
And there are ways it
can continue to change

00:15:01.290 --> 00:15:02.670
and they can move through it.

00:15:02.670 --> 00:15:04.410
So I think for me, it was helpful to see

00:15:04.410 --> 00:15:06.870
how they also processed the information,

00:15:06.870 --> 00:15:09.150
and then incorporate it
into their understanding.

00:15:09.150 --> 00:15:10.020
And then I would say for me,

00:15:10.020 --> 00:15:12.420
bringing that back into the lab setting,

00:15:12.420 --> 00:15:13.680
even for us who are in the lab,

00:15:13.680 --> 00:15:15.900
I think it also gives people perspective.

00:15:15.900 --> 00:15:18.810
Like why we do what we
do, what impact it has,

00:15:18.810 --> 00:15:19.710
and not just thinking about

00:15:19.710 --> 00:15:21.840
within the specific lab
environment as well.

00:15:21.840 --> 00:15:24.510
So, I think all the way around
those things are so important

00:15:24.510 --> 00:15:26.430
from a teaching perspective.

00:15:26.430 --> 00:15:27.420
And obviously we're trying to make sure

00:15:27.420 --> 00:15:30.210
that we don't keep these
conversations stuck

00:15:30.210 --> 00:15:32.730
in certain silos, in certain venues.

00:15:32.730 --> 00:15:34.680
In a lot of ways, that's
why I'm so grateful

00:15:34.680 --> 00:15:37.010
for the way that your work touches all

00:15:37.010 --> 00:15:38.220
of these different components.

00:15:38.220 --> 00:15:39.690
Even as you talked about in the beginning,

00:15:39.690 --> 00:15:42.000
the administrative roles,
the teaching roles,

00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:43.920
the work in the clinic, you're
doing a lot in the community,

00:15:43.920 --> 00:15:46.350
I think that's so
important and so powerful.

00:15:46.350 --> 00:15:47.913
- Thank you, I appreciate that.

00:15:51.750 --> 00:15:55.200
- It's important and hopefully
that feels gratifying.

00:15:55.200 --> 00:15:57.180
'Cause I know that when we're
in the work in the moment,

00:15:57.180 --> 00:15:59.820
it doesn't always feel that
way, at least maybe more so

00:15:59.820 --> 00:16:01.170
for you in the clinic, but.

00:16:02.026 --> 00:16:03.270
- You know, that's really interesting.

00:16:03.270 --> 00:16:06.600
So hearing you talk about

00:16:06.600 --> 00:16:08.370
how you can bring those experiences

00:16:08.370 --> 00:16:13.370
with the community back to
the lab, that's really moving

00:16:14.280 --> 00:16:19.280
for me because I do think
about, as you said, these silos

00:16:19.470 --> 00:16:24.470
and that research can feel
so disconnected from sort

00:16:26.520 --> 00:16:29.880
of like the day to day sort of realities,

00:16:29.880 --> 00:16:32.010
whether it's of someone's
life and their addiction,

00:16:32.010 --> 00:16:35.520
and even the challenges between

00:16:35.520 --> 00:16:36.990
what happens in a research world

00:16:36.990 --> 00:16:39.450
and what's happening
in a clinical setting.

00:16:39.450 --> 00:16:42.750
And that the communication across all

00:16:42.750 --> 00:16:45.570
of those settings is
really, really important

00:16:45.570 --> 00:16:49.830
to keep patient experiences at the center,

00:16:49.830 --> 00:16:53.190
that our research is
informed by those experiences

00:16:53.190 --> 00:16:56.040
so that it can help us
do better clinical work.

00:16:56.040 --> 00:16:59.160
It's like we need the research to inform

00:16:59.160 --> 00:17:02.610
what we're doing in the clinic,
so we can be more effective.

00:17:02.610 --> 00:17:05.820
But again, if we're not
really listening in terms

00:17:05.820 --> 00:17:08.040
of what the patient experience is,

00:17:08.040 --> 00:17:11.070
we may be focusing on a really interesting

00:17:11.070 --> 00:17:13.860
and novel sort of research finding,

00:17:13.860 --> 00:17:17.460
but how do we bring
that back to the person

00:17:17.460 --> 00:17:20.040
who's like struggling and still trying

00:17:20.040 --> 00:17:22.380
to figure out their path
and their way forward.

00:17:22.380 --> 00:17:27.380
So that really excites me to
see that those conversations

00:17:27.717 --> 00:17:30.993
and that engagement is happening
across various sectors.

00:17:32.154 --> 00:17:37.154
I am reminded in that same way,

00:17:41.340 --> 00:17:43.500
even in sort of my day to day work,

00:17:43.500 --> 00:17:46.920
it's not something that
I think about explicitly,

00:17:46.920 --> 00:17:49.980
but in this conversation it's reminding me

00:17:49.980 --> 00:17:52.560
that often the challenges that I'm trying

00:17:52.560 --> 00:17:54.690
to work out in an
administrative setting have

00:17:54.690 --> 00:17:57.573
to do with barriers that
I've seen my patients face.

00:17:58.500 --> 00:18:02.550
Like that I'm realizing this
is a wall we keep running into.

00:18:02.550 --> 00:18:04.890
And so how can we adjust our systems

00:18:04.890 --> 00:18:06.210
and our practices,

00:18:06.210 --> 00:18:09.390
so that my patients aren't
always running into this wall.

00:18:09.390 --> 00:18:11.670
- Yeah, that's such an
important perspective.

00:18:11.670 --> 00:18:13.770
I imagine you might
have to remind yourself

00:18:13.770 --> 00:18:17.610
of that a few times as
you go through just yeah,

00:18:17.610 --> 00:18:20.880
'cause it's easy, so easy
to lose sight of that.

00:18:20.880 --> 00:18:22.500
I think as much as we can remember

00:18:22.500 --> 00:18:24.270
that across the board in different things

00:18:24.270 --> 00:18:26.130
that we're doing is important.

00:18:26.130 --> 00:18:26.963
And I would say, you know,

00:18:26.963 --> 00:18:29.970
in some ways it's still surprising to me

00:18:29.970 --> 00:18:32.520
and I guess it shouldn't be,
but sometimes it surprises me,

00:18:32.520 --> 00:18:34.590
the impact that things can have.

00:18:34.590 --> 00:18:37.620
So I definitely agree as we need

00:18:37.620 --> 00:18:38.910
to move things on a system level,

00:18:38.910 --> 00:18:41.130
but even individually sometimes.

00:18:41.130 --> 00:18:43.380
So I remember one talk I did
when I was just talking about,

00:18:43.380 --> 00:18:45.390
again, the basic science in the lab,

00:18:45.390 --> 00:18:47.220
and talking about some
experiments that we're trying

00:18:47.220 --> 00:18:49.620
to, you know, do in rats or mice to try

00:18:49.620 --> 00:18:51.210
and model addiction or depression,

00:18:51.210 --> 00:18:53.070
which obviously you
can't do all that easily.

00:18:53.070 --> 00:18:55.710
But trying to think about this
back and forth translation.

00:18:55.710 --> 00:18:58.350
And having feedback from people who said,

00:18:58.350 --> 00:19:01.710
as you described what was
happening in a clinical situation,

00:19:01.710 --> 00:19:02.880
how you tried to model that,

00:19:02.880 --> 00:19:04.770
and how you tried to go back and forth,

00:19:04.770 --> 00:19:06.900
they said even that gave them insight into

00:19:06.900 --> 00:19:10.230
what they had seen their
loved ones navigate

00:19:10.230 --> 00:19:11.730
with substance use disorders.

00:19:11.730 --> 00:19:12.909
- Wow.

00:19:12.909 --> 00:19:13.742
- To me, that was surprising

00:19:13.742 --> 00:19:15.330
because I don't often think
about it in that sense.

00:19:15.330 --> 00:19:16.290
Even though we're trying to model,

00:19:16.290 --> 00:19:17.940
I don't think about what
are people actually going

00:19:17.940 --> 00:19:21.210
to get from thinking about
what we're saying about

00:19:21.210 --> 00:19:23.640
what happens in the brain and
how an animal might behave

00:19:23.640 --> 00:19:25.650
that might give them
insight and understanding

00:19:25.650 --> 00:19:29.010
to how their loved one might
be acting in a certain way.

00:19:29.010 --> 00:19:30.660
So that to me was really powerful.

00:19:30.660 --> 00:19:33.570
And I don't think that's
a common experience

00:19:33.570 --> 00:19:36.360
that we think about if we're
talking about the science,

00:19:36.360 --> 00:19:38.400
that it can actually
have a direct application

00:19:38.400 --> 00:19:39.570
to how somebody thinks about

00:19:39.570 --> 00:19:41.880
how someone they know is responding

00:19:41.880 --> 00:19:44.070
and how to also approach that person,

00:19:44.070 --> 00:19:46.500
having that understanding
about, okay, well this might

00:19:46.500 --> 00:19:47.490
be happening.

00:19:47.490 --> 00:19:50.547
This set of pathways may be
active in their brain this way.

00:19:50.547 --> 00:19:52.560
And that may be causing
them to react in this way.

00:19:52.560 --> 00:19:54.870
And maybe I should enter
into that in a different way.

00:19:54.870 --> 00:19:58.680
So to me, that was just shocking
because I didn't see it,

00:19:58.680 --> 00:19:59.513
even though you would hope

00:19:59.513 --> 00:20:01.935
that I would think about that, but.

00:20:01.935 --> 00:20:04.380
- But so what you're describing,

00:20:04.380 --> 00:20:07.743
and what excites me about that,

00:20:11.700 --> 00:20:14.610
and there are lots of folks
in the field of addiction

00:20:14.610 --> 00:20:16.350
who have been really trying

00:20:16.350 --> 00:20:19.770
to push this message in terms
of the science of addiction,

00:20:19.770 --> 00:20:24.770
helping us to combat stigma
because the behaviors

00:20:27.000 --> 00:20:31.290
of a person with a substance
use disorder are so hard

00:20:31.290 --> 00:20:33.840
to understand, right?

00:20:33.840 --> 00:20:34.673
So you're just like,

00:20:34.673 --> 00:20:37.230
you're talking about you're
discussing this moment

00:20:37.230 --> 00:20:40.410
with a family member who
started to have an understanding

00:20:40.410 --> 00:20:45.270
why their loved one might
respond in a certain way.

00:20:45.270 --> 00:20:49.230
And that in and of itself,

00:20:49.230 --> 00:20:51.360
because the behaviors
are hard to understand,

00:20:51.360 --> 00:20:53.520
then they are stigmatized.

00:20:53.520 --> 00:20:54.840
And it's like, the science

00:20:54.840 --> 00:20:58.350
of addiction can help us understand like

00:20:58.350 --> 00:21:02.010
what is actually driving
some of those behaviors

00:21:02.010 --> 00:21:05.040
that other people find hard to understand.

00:21:05.040 --> 00:21:06.810
When we don't understand something,

00:21:06.810 --> 00:21:09.780
we make it an other in
whatever way that is.

00:21:09.780 --> 00:21:10.980
It's different from us.

00:21:10.980 --> 00:21:12.210
It doesn't make sense.

00:21:12.210 --> 00:21:14.343
And so we distance ourselves from it.

00:21:15.210 --> 00:21:18.750
But bringing that understanding
in a very practical way,

00:21:18.750 --> 00:21:22.980
I think it really does help
us to combat the stigma

00:21:22.980 --> 00:21:27.330
and helps us to help families
and individuals understand

00:21:27.330 --> 00:21:31.080
why certain changes are happening
in the way someone engages

00:21:31.080 --> 00:21:32.886
with their world.

00:21:32.886 --> 00:21:35.340
- Yeah, that's really encouraging.

00:21:35.340 --> 00:21:37.410
Now it's my turn 'cause I'm gonna have

00:21:37.410 --> 00:21:39.510
to remind myself of that too.

00:21:39.510 --> 00:21:40.560
You know, just in the day to day

00:21:40.560 --> 00:21:42.660
'cause it's so easy to lose sight of that.

00:21:43.710 --> 00:21:45.180
To follow up on that, I
mean, since you brought up,

00:21:45.180 --> 00:21:47.370
I mean appropriately this aspect of stigma

00:21:47.370 --> 00:21:49.290
and how the science can inform that,

00:21:49.290 --> 00:21:52.320
how do you approach
that in your day to day,

00:21:52.320 --> 00:21:53.730
even though, you know,
there's a range of things

00:21:53.730 --> 00:21:55.050
that encompass your day to day,

00:21:55.050 --> 00:21:56.550
but how have you tried to navigate

00:21:56.550 --> 00:21:58.710
and help people move past the stigma

00:21:58.710 --> 00:22:00.630
so they can get to the
place where they are coming

00:22:00.630 --> 00:22:03.750
to get the treatments that you
have mentioned are effective?

00:22:03.750 --> 00:22:05.730
- Yeah, it's really interesting.

00:22:05.730 --> 00:22:10.080
So I find that stigma, it
acts on so many levels,

00:22:10.080 --> 00:22:12.273
especially when it comes to substance use.

00:22:13.392 --> 00:22:18.392
There's an internalized stigma
where individuals wonder

00:22:23.220 --> 00:22:24.670
whether or not they're worthy

00:22:26.250 --> 00:22:28.380
because they've been treated in a way

00:22:28.380 --> 00:22:30.530
that makes them feel
as though they're not.

00:22:32.040 --> 00:22:36.240
And that can happen at various
stages of someone's recovery.

00:22:36.240 --> 00:22:40.590
You know, someone who's been
abstinent from substances

00:22:40.590 --> 00:22:43.770
for a period of time,

00:22:43.770 --> 00:22:47.520
but they're still working
on rebuilding relationships

00:22:47.520 --> 00:22:51.780
that have been broken, and
for them to really know

00:22:51.780 --> 00:22:54.063
that they're worthy of
healthy relationships,

00:22:55.080 --> 00:22:57.600
to overcome an internalized stigma about

00:22:57.600 --> 00:23:01.743
what kind of life they
are worthy of having.

00:23:03.180 --> 00:23:07.020
And again, it happens along
the spectrum of recovery.

00:23:07.020 --> 00:23:11.140
Where I find stigma to be
most challenging though is

00:23:12.060 --> 00:23:15.723
in the systems that are
meant to serve our patients.

00:23:17.280 --> 00:23:18.113
- Wow.

00:23:18.113 --> 00:23:22.457
- And that is the part that
could be really discouraging.

00:23:25.980 --> 00:23:29.010
it's definitely discouraging
for patients when they try

00:23:29.010 --> 00:23:30.090
to access help.

00:23:30.090 --> 00:23:34.203
And then when they finally
sort of make that decision

00:23:36.210 --> 00:23:40.690
and have the courage to make
a choice towards treatment

00:23:41.790 --> 00:23:46.710
and they're met by stigmatizing behaviors

00:23:46.710 --> 00:23:49.323
and language from care providers.

00:23:50.580 --> 00:23:54.633
That's the thing that really
makes my job more difficult.

00:23:55.500 --> 00:23:59.130
And so I'm very careful in my language.

00:23:59.130 --> 00:24:01.830
I model language, even
in the health record,

00:24:01.830 --> 00:24:05.613
as I talk about patients and
as I describe their behaviors.

00:24:07.009 --> 00:24:11.372
I use person-first language.

00:24:11.372 --> 00:24:16.372
I prefer terms like
abstinence rather than sort

00:24:16.650 --> 00:24:19.383
of clean or dirty, right?

00:24:20.370 --> 00:24:23.130
So these are some of the practical things

00:24:23.130 --> 00:24:28.130
that I implement in my daily
work to combat that stigma.

00:24:28.290 --> 00:24:32.124
And that I'm humanizing someone
when I'm talking about them,

00:24:32.124 --> 00:24:36.630
even in my clinical
documentation that I want

00:24:36.630 --> 00:24:40.740
to make sure we see this
individual as a whole person

00:24:40.740 --> 00:24:44.283
and not only as someone with an addiction.

00:24:47.280 --> 00:24:50.370
So that's where I find myself.

00:24:50.370 --> 00:24:55.370
That's the space I find myself
in most often, modeling it,

00:24:55.890 --> 00:24:59.880
trying to do some education
with my colleagues

00:24:59.880 --> 00:25:02.280
and peers and coworkers.

00:25:02.280 --> 00:25:07.280
Some of the clinical research
on stigma demonstrates

00:25:08.280 --> 00:25:13.280
that using stigmatizing
language directly correlates

00:25:13.680 --> 00:25:18.150
to choosing more punitive
sort of responses to patients.

00:25:18.150 --> 00:25:20.940
So it informs the way we decide

00:25:20.940 --> 00:25:25.710
to treat someone if we
use stigmatizing language.

00:25:25.710 --> 00:25:28.800
And if we read stigmatizing
language in the chart,

00:25:28.800 --> 00:25:31.170
it informs how we view that person

00:25:31.170 --> 00:25:33.330
and what we think they're
worthy of in terms

00:25:33.330 --> 00:25:34.620
of their treatment.

00:25:34.620 --> 00:25:39.620
So it really is important
how we talk about addiction,

00:25:39.960 --> 00:25:43.410
how we talk amongst ourselves
as clinical providers

00:25:43.410 --> 00:25:47.280
and then what that looks
like in a patient-facing way.

00:25:47.280 --> 00:25:50.190
- Yeah, wow, that's huge and so important.

00:25:50.190 --> 00:25:51.810
Where do you think, and
this might be speculative,

00:25:51.810 --> 00:25:54.150
where do you think that
comes from in terms

00:25:54.150 --> 00:25:56.790
of within those settings, the fact that

00:25:56.790 --> 00:25:58.530
that stigmatizing language is still there?

00:25:58.530 --> 00:26:00.540
And maybe another question on top of that,

00:26:00.540 --> 00:26:04.860
do you think it's intentional
or is it something

00:26:04.860 --> 00:26:06.600
that's not even realized,

00:26:06.600 --> 00:26:09.060
just the way that things have been done?

00:26:09.060 --> 00:26:10.263
- That's interesting.

00:26:11.490 --> 00:26:16.490
So this would be speculative,

00:26:16.590 --> 00:26:21.163
but my thought is that we
have systems and structures

00:26:24.960 --> 00:26:27.663
that actually stigmatize our patients,

00:26:27.663 --> 00:26:30.750
that some of the stigma is
written into policies in terms

00:26:30.750 --> 00:26:33.270
of the ways that we treat individuals

00:26:33.270 --> 00:26:34.953
with substance use disorder,

00:26:35.850 --> 00:26:39.540
the history of equating substance use

00:26:39.540 --> 00:26:43.710
with criminality in such a deep, deep way,

00:26:43.710 --> 00:26:48.710
and that those policies,

00:26:48.810 --> 00:26:53.810
and that those media
portrayals going back decades,

00:26:54.570 --> 00:26:58.710
really inform how people
think about someone

00:26:58.710 --> 00:27:00.090
with an addiction.

00:27:00.090 --> 00:27:05.090
And they see them as sort of
in the worst case scenarios

00:27:06.270 --> 00:27:08.883
as sort of a crazed criminal.

00:27:10.350 --> 00:27:15.350
And those public sort of media portrayals,

00:27:15.720 --> 00:27:18.150
whether you're in a
clinical setting or not,

00:27:18.150 --> 00:27:20.583
that's just in the water in society.

00:27:22.200 --> 00:27:27.200
And it takes change in
multiple levels and sectors

00:27:29.370 --> 00:27:33.930
to really shift how we
are perceiving patients

00:27:33.930 --> 00:27:36.150
and individuals with a
substance use disorder

00:27:36.150 --> 00:27:38.700
in society overall,

00:27:38.700 --> 00:27:41.733
and then also what that looks
like in medical settings.

00:27:43.239 --> 00:27:47.820
They're thought of as the
sort of difficult patient.

00:27:47.820 --> 00:27:51.120
And because it may take more time

00:27:51.120 --> 00:27:56.120
to understand their
behavior, right, then again,

00:27:56.790 --> 00:28:00.420
even in a medical
setting, walls are put up

00:28:00.420 --> 00:28:03.990
to say that's the challenging patient,

00:28:03.990 --> 00:28:05.610
that's the difficult patient.

00:28:05.610 --> 00:28:10.170
And conscious or unconscious,
we have these responses.

00:28:10.170 --> 00:28:11.610
- Yeah, and then, like you mentioned,

00:28:11.610 --> 00:28:13.950
it has an impact on how
things are perceived

00:28:13.950 --> 00:28:14.970
when it's written into the chart.

00:28:14.970 --> 00:28:16.860
And then to add on top of that,

00:28:16.860 --> 00:28:19.170
when you think about our
Black and brown communities,

00:28:19.170 --> 00:28:20.610
that's a whole nother level.

00:28:20.610 --> 00:28:23.010
So I mean, what have you seen around

00:28:23.010 --> 00:28:24.930
that either from your observations

00:28:24.930 --> 00:28:27.420
or, I mean, from what's been
sort of researched as well?

00:28:27.420 --> 00:28:30.480
Like how do those do two
things intersect in terms

00:28:30.480 --> 00:28:33.840
of the language that's used
and people's background?

00:28:33.840 --> 00:28:36.660
- Yeah, that's a great question.

00:28:36.660 --> 00:28:38.820
So Black patients,

00:28:38.820 --> 00:28:41.220
regardless of their
diagnosis are more likely

00:28:41.220 --> 00:28:45.423
to be labeled aggressive,
right, in medical documentation.

00:28:46.440 --> 00:28:50.580
And so when you add in the components

00:28:50.580 --> 00:28:52.560
of substance use disorder, right,

00:28:52.560 --> 00:28:55.410
if someone is withdrawing
from a substance, right,

00:28:55.410 --> 00:28:57.900
and that impacts their behavior.

00:28:57.900 --> 00:29:00.990
They're physically
uncomfortable to the point

00:29:00.990 --> 00:29:04.200
that they feel like, you
know, for some substances,

00:29:04.200 --> 00:29:06.240
the withdrawal feels like you're dying.

00:29:06.240 --> 00:29:10.320
Like you're in such discomfort

00:29:10.320 --> 00:29:13.950
that it is overwhelming, right?

00:29:13.950 --> 00:29:18.950
And so if a Black male comes
into a medical setting in

00:29:21.810 --> 00:29:25.950
that level of discomfort and he's yelling,

00:29:25.950 --> 00:29:28.713
and saying, I need help right now, right,

00:29:35.490 --> 00:29:40.140
it doesn't engender these
sort of caregiving responses

00:29:40.140 --> 00:29:42.600
from the clinical staff, right?

00:29:42.600 --> 00:29:47.600
And we know how implicit bias works.

00:29:50.250 --> 00:29:53.280
Another aspect of this is
how burnt out individuals

00:29:53.280 --> 00:29:54.510
in the healthcare system are,

00:29:54.510 --> 00:29:57.480
which means they're more likely
to respond based on bias.

00:29:57.480 --> 00:29:59.790
So in the middle of an overdose crisis,

00:29:59.790 --> 00:30:02.550
that's disproportionately
affecting Black communities

00:30:02.550 --> 00:30:06.210
and communities of color,
right, we need caregiving spaces

00:30:06.210 --> 00:30:08.580
to be welcoming and affirming and safe

00:30:08.580 --> 00:30:13.113
for people to engage truly so
that we can be saving lives.

00:30:17.190 --> 00:30:19.020
But Black individuals coming

00:30:19.020 --> 00:30:23.310
into seeking care are less
likely to receive medications

00:30:23.310 --> 00:30:27.930
for opioid use disorder, even
in emergency room settings,

00:30:27.930 --> 00:30:30.420
which is a critical point of intervention

00:30:30.420 --> 00:30:32.823
when we're thinking about
the overdose crisis.

00:30:33.710 --> 00:30:36.030
So the data is showing

00:30:36.030 --> 00:30:39.390
that the clinical behavior
towards communities

00:30:39.390 --> 00:30:43.743
of color is different when it
comes to addiction treatment.

00:30:44.580 --> 00:30:49.580
And I think it's the
interplay of stigma, racism,

00:30:50.850 --> 00:30:52.203
and racial bias.

00:30:53.760 --> 00:30:55.410
Those things in combination

00:30:55.410 --> 00:30:59.760
with one another really contribute

00:30:59.760 --> 00:31:04.545
to the difference in care that
is offered or not offered.

00:31:04.545 --> 00:31:06.840
- Yeah, I mean, even as I'm
listening to you talking,

00:31:06.840 --> 00:31:08.850
I'm just thinking about all the impacts

00:31:08.850 --> 00:31:11.190
that has on the outcomes.

00:31:11.190 --> 00:31:12.690
When I'm thinking, I mean,
it's giving me a level

00:31:12.690 --> 00:31:15.990
of empathy on both sides,
both for the person

00:31:15.990 --> 00:31:17.160
who is walking through that,

00:31:17.160 --> 00:31:18.540
if they feel like they're dying,

00:31:18.540 --> 00:31:21.570
then also for those who
may not be responding

00:31:21.570 --> 00:31:24.060
to that in the appropriate
manner because of biases

00:31:24.060 --> 00:31:25.290
or because of being burnt out.

00:31:25.290 --> 00:31:27.180
And again, I'm thinking about
the neuroscience of that.

00:31:27.180 --> 00:31:30.720
And if both situations, both
individuals are at a high level

00:31:30.720 --> 00:31:33.510
of stress, how that
just can amplify things,

00:31:33.510 --> 00:31:36.687
but then it has such
tremendous impacts on outcomes.

00:31:36.687 --> 00:31:38.760
And I'm even thinking, as
you talked about in terms

00:31:38.760 --> 00:31:39.780
of overdose, I mean, that's a life

00:31:39.780 --> 00:31:43.890
or death, ultimately,
situation, potentially.

00:31:43.890 --> 00:31:46.260
So again, I mean, just
everything you brought up is

00:31:46.260 --> 00:31:50.100
just giving me time to
pause and just realize

00:31:50.100 --> 00:31:52.470
how important all these
different components are

00:31:52.470 --> 00:31:54.180
at all these different levels.

00:31:54.180 --> 00:31:55.860
And as you mentioned before,
it's something that has

00:31:55.860 --> 00:31:59.040
to happen on a systems level as well.

00:31:59.040 --> 00:32:02.220
So lots to do, but again,
I'm grateful for the ways

00:32:02.220 --> 00:32:03.360
that you're doing that.

00:32:03.360 --> 00:32:05.370
In some ways, I think
even this conversation

00:32:05.370 --> 00:32:06.930
we're having is giving us some insights

00:32:06.930 --> 00:32:11.220
about different areas where
many of us can maybe intervene

00:32:11.220 --> 00:32:12.150
and be proactive

00:32:12.150 --> 00:32:14.298
and make sure that we have
a better understanding

00:32:14.298 --> 00:32:16.350
of those types of things as well.

00:32:16.350 --> 00:32:17.850
But again, it makes me just grateful

00:32:17.850 --> 00:32:21.090
for the work that you do and
for the fact that you continue

00:32:21.090 --> 00:32:22.500
to do it as well.

00:32:22.500 --> 00:32:23.460
'Cause I think it's a mixture.

00:32:23.460 --> 00:32:26.250
There's a lot of hope there,
but it can also be easy

00:32:26.250 --> 00:32:29.040
to have some aspect of despair

00:32:29.040 --> 00:32:32.130
or is this actually making a bigger impact

00:32:32.130 --> 00:32:32.963
and a bigger difference,

00:32:32.963 --> 00:32:35.090
which I imagine can
come into play as well.

00:32:37.140 --> 00:32:38.610
- I appreciate that.

00:32:38.610 --> 00:32:43.053
I was just gonna say when it
comes to maintaining hope,

00:32:44.460 --> 00:32:48.390
that also comes with my
interactions with my patients.

00:32:48.390 --> 00:32:49.983
That's how I maintain hope,

00:32:52.679 --> 00:32:55.710
when we have a moment
and we're recognizing

00:32:55.710 --> 00:32:57.690
that things are working,

00:32:57.690 --> 00:33:00.480
and we get to celebrate that together.

00:33:00.480 --> 00:33:02.610
Like that is just powerful.

00:33:02.610 --> 00:33:04.740
I had one of those moments this week

00:33:04.740 --> 00:33:06.450
and I want to share.

00:33:13.530 --> 00:33:16.560
And I'm just thinking about
the best way to do that.

00:33:16.560 --> 00:33:20.490
And so, but this is a particular patient

00:33:20.490 --> 00:33:24.810
that has alcohol use disorder
and really, really struggling

00:33:24.810 --> 00:33:29.010
with alcohol use disorder,
severe withdrawal,

00:33:29.010 --> 00:33:32.430
severe medical complications
of alcohol use,

00:33:32.430 --> 00:33:36.750
including pancreatitis
requiring stays in the ICU

00:33:36.750 --> 00:33:41.750
for the withdrawal, just
really, really very, very sick.

00:33:44.280 --> 00:33:48.090
And we had tried various medications

00:33:48.090 --> 00:33:50.130
to help in maintaining abstinence,

00:33:50.130 --> 00:33:52.500
but that wasn't really working.

00:33:52.500 --> 00:33:56.010
And so we started just talking
through a plan of, well,

00:33:56.010 --> 00:33:59.553
what does it look like to
cut down on your alcohol use,

00:34:00.690 --> 00:34:03.510
on your own with all of the engagement

00:34:03.510 --> 00:34:06.120
and various treatment
resources, coming to groups,

00:34:06.120 --> 00:34:11.120
doing a little bit of AA, just
multiple different supports.

00:34:12.720 --> 00:34:16.410
And for the first time the patient came

00:34:16.410 --> 00:34:19.320
into my office after
not having had alcohol

00:34:19.320 --> 00:34:20.760
for a couple of days

00:34:20.760 --> 00:34:22.890
and there were no physical
signs of withdrawal,

00:34:22.890 --> 00:34:25.800
like blood pressure was normal.

00:34:25.800 --> 00:34:28.740
And all of this came from sort

00:34:28.740 --> 00:34:30.420
of really harm reduction principles

00:34:30.420 --> 00:34:33.240
and slowly thinking about
how this person wanted

00:34:33.240 --> 00:34:35.190
to decrease their alcohol use

00:34:35.190 --> 00:34:38.250
because medications just
hadn't been working.

00:34:38.250 --> 00:34:42.240
And it was the first time
that their body like actually

00:34:42.240 --> 00:34:47.240
was showing a physical response
that the plan was working.

00:34:47.280 --> 00:34:50.370
So I was just like, look,
this is it in black and white.

00:34:50.370 --> 00:34:51.600
We're making progress.

00:34:51.600 --> 00:34:52.830
This is good.

00:34:52.830 --> 00:34:55.683
So made my week.

00:34:57.286 --> 00:34:58.119
It made my week.

00:34:58.119 --> 00:34:58.952
- That's great.

00:34:58.952 --> 00:34:59.785
I mean, definitely the hope there,

00:34:59.785 --> 00:35:01.830
but definitely I'm sensing the joy too

00:35:01.830 --> 00:35:04.410
that comes from seeing that shift.

00:35:04.410 --> 00:35:05.243
- Yes.

00:35:06.330 --> 00:35:07.290
- And celebrating it.

00:35:07.290 --> 00:35:09.360
- And celebrating, you have to.

00:35:09.360 --> 00:35:12.480
And there are some who might
look at that from the outside

00:35:12.480 --> 00:35:13.560
and they're thinking well,

00:35:13.560 --> 00:35:16.890
but the person's not fully
abstinent from alcohol, right?

00:35:16.890 --> 00:35:20.463
And so what does that mean?

00:35:21.480 --> 00:35:25.260
And here I am saying,
no, we take every win,

00:35:25.260 --> 00:35:27.480
and we celebrate every win.

00:35:27.480 --> 00:35:30.450
And that is worth celebrating.

00:35:30.450 --> 00:35:32.700
- Yeah, fully agree.

00:35:32.700 --> 00:35:34.110
I appreciate you sharing that as well.

00:35:34.110 --> 00:35:38.520
Again, that joy just is so
evident in a lot of ways.

00:35:38.520 --> 00:35:40.440
- Yeah, and that's what sustains me,

00:35:40.440 --> 00:35:44.760
those moments where I can celebrate

00:35:44.760 --> 00:35:48.669
with my patient in the
progress that they're making.

00:35:48.669 --> 00:35:52.020
- Yeah, well, that's so impactful,

00:35:52.020 --> 00:35:55.140
and so many aspects of you,
you know, being a conduit

00:35:55.140 --> 00:35:57.540
to give hope in a lot of ways.

00:35:57.540 --> 00:36:00.510
And, you know, just to kind
of wrap in the faith component

00:36:00.510 --> 00:36:02.603
as well, knowing that
you're a person of faith,

00:36:02.603 --> 00:36:05.700
you know, as we talk
about this topic of hope,

00:36:05.700 --> 00:36:08.460
how does all of that intersect
with all, maybe not all,

00:36:08.460 --> 00:36:10.410
maybe that's too many,
but many of these things?

00:36:10.410 --> 00:36:11.697
It does intersect with all,

00:36:11.697 --> 00:36:13.770
but I don't know if you have
time to go into all of that,

00:36:13.770 --> 00:36:17.340
but with many of the topics
that we've been talking about,

00:36:17.340 --> 00:36:20.970
and then in just your
perspective in your day to day?

00:36:20.970 --> 00:36:25.970
- Yeah, you know, it does come back

00:36:26.550 --> 00:36:30.060
to that idea of the ministry of presence.

00:36:30.060 --> 00:36:33.813
I think that being present with someone,

00:36:37.761 --> 00:36:41.643
you can't undersell
the importance of that,

00:36:43.920 --> 00:36:48.780
and like to really,
really sit with someone

00:36:48.780 --> 00:36:51.273
and to let them know
that they've been heard.

00:36:54.300 --> 00:36:59.300
And the medical system can
keep you busy seeing patients

00:37:00.810 --> 00:37:02.820
at a really rapid pace.

00:37:02.820 --> 00:37:06.480
And, you know, I do my best to minimize

00:37:06.480 --> 00:37:10.260
that in my schedule as much as I can.

00:37:10.260 --> 00:37:14.970
And I'm grateful to work in a
space that allows me a measure

00:37:14.970 --> 00:37:18.030
of flexibility to be able to do that.

00:37:18.030 --> 00:37:19.890
'Cause I recognize that that
doesn't happen everywhere

00:37:19.890 --> 00:37:22.590
and the need is so great
that there are so many people

00:37:22.590 --> 00:37:23.733
that need to be seen,

00:37:24.870 --> 00:37:27.990
and it can leave you just
running from one room

00:37:27.990 --> 00:37:29.890
to the next, to the next, to the next.

00:37:30.900 --> 00:37:33.150
So I do my best to do a little bit

00:37:33.150 --> 00:37:37.140
of a self check-in before I
go in a room with the patient

00:37:37.140 --> 00:37:41.643
to be sure that I can
actually be present with them

00:37:41.643 --> 00:37:44.790
and not just kind of go
through a symptom checklist,

00:37:44.790 --> 00:37:47.733
but to really, really
actually be able to hear them.

00:37:48.930 --> 00:37:53.930
And I know that that really does come from

00:37:54.060 --> 00:37:57.963
what I've experienced in my walk with God.

00:38:00.570 --> 00:38:04.340
The importance of knowing
that He never leaves

00:38:07.770 --> 00:38:11.583
and never forsakes you like
that He is present with you.

00:38:14.640 --> 00:38:19.640
I know how radically that
transforms my daily outlook

00:38:21.630 --> 00:38:23.673
to know that He is with me.

00:38:24.540 --> 00:38:29.540
And I just feel that there is a way,

00:38:31.620 --> 00:38:35.040
and certainly I'm not talking about God

00:38:35.040 --> 00:38:38.370
in my clinical encounters,
unless a patient brings that up

00:38:38.370 --> 00:38:42.060
to me that that's an important
part of their recovery.

00:38:42.060 --> 00:38:44.790
And for many patients, it is.

00:38:44.790 --> 00:38:46.950
For some patients, it's not at all.

00:38:46.950 --> 00:38:49.980
I have a patient who happened
to find me on Twitter

00:38:49.980 --> 00:38:53.310
and told me how he realized
that I was a spiritual person

00:38:53.310 --> 00:38:54.787
and he's not really,

00:38:54.787 --> 00:38:58.569
and we had, you know, a quick
little discussion about that.

00:38:58.569 --> 00:39:01.320
- [Nii] It's a good, honest conversation.

00:39:01.320 --> 00:39:06.320
- But just as an example
of the ways in which,

00:39:06.510 --> 00:39:08.973
you know, I'm mindful of those boundaries.

00:39:10.050 --> 00:39:15.050
And at the same time, I do
feel that it is an extension

00:39:18.180 --> 00:39:22.353
of God's grace to be
present with someone else,

00:39:24.570 --> 00:39:29.340
and to just sit and hear,
and to hear without judgment

00:39:29.340 --> 00:39:33.150
and to let someone know that
they've been understood.

00:39:33.150 --> 00:39:38.150
And I'm not able to do that
every day and every visit

00:39:39.000 --> 00:39:41.373
'cause it's busy and it's hard,

00:39:42.870 --> 00:39:46.980
but I try to take a moment to be mindful.

00:39:46.980 --> 00:39:49.680
If I'm having a stressful
day and I know it's gonna

00:39:49.680 --> 00:39:52.500
be a little harder than
usual to just be present

